<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-finance-savings-plans" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Annuities and Sinking Funds</title>

  <!-- This section is a remix of PCC, Sekhon and Bloom material, and my own original writing. -->

  <objectives>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Find the future value of an annuity.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Determine the payment needed to create a sinking fund with a specified value.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      For most of us it is not practical to deposit a large sum of money in the bank. Instead, we save by depositing smaller amounts of money regularly. We might save in an IRA or 401(k) for retirement. We might also save for a down payment on a car or house, or in a college savings plan for our children, or make regular payments into an insurance account.
    </p>
    <p>
      A sequence of equal periodic payments is called an <term>annuity</term>.<idx>annuities</idx> The payments (or deposits) can be made annually, quarterly, monthly, or at any other fixed interval of time. When the payments are made at the same time the interest is credited to the account, the annuity is called a <term>simple</term> annuity.<idx><h>annuities</h><h>simple</h></idx> When these payments are at the end of the payment period, the annuity is called <term>ordinary</term>.<idx><h>annuities</h><h>ordinary</h></idx>
      There are many other types of annuities, but we will not consider them here. This text will only discuss simple, ordinary annuities with equal-sized payments. 
    </p>
    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-finance-compounding-tip">
      <title>Tip</title>
      <p>
        If the compounding frequency is not explicitly stated in a problem, assume that the number of compounding periods per year is the same as the number of deposits per year.
      </p>
    </assemblage>
  </introduction>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-annuities">
    <title>Future Value of an Annuity</title>
    <p>
      The mathematical formula needed to solve problems involving the future value of simple, ordinary annuities is shown below. If you want to know where the formula comes from, it is derived at the end of this section in <xref ref="subsec-derivation-future-value-of-annuity"/>.
    </p>
    <assemblage>
      <title>Future Value of an Ordinary Annuity</title>
      <idx>future value of an annuity</idx>
      <idx><h>financial formulas</h><h>future value of an annuity</h></idx>
      <idx><h>annuities</h><h>future value</h></idx>
      <p>
        If a regular payment of <m>d</m> dollars is made in an account <m>n</m> times a year, and the account earns an annual interest rate <m>r</m> compounded <m>n</m> times per year, then the future value <m>A</m> of the annuity after <m>t</m> years is
        <md>
          A = d \left[\frac{\left(1+\frac{r}{n}\right)^{nt}-1}{\frac{r}{n}}\right].
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Glossary:
      </p>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p><m>A</m> is the balance or amount in the account after <m>t</m> years (the future value)</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p><m>d</m> is the regular deposit (or payment amount each month, quarter, year, etc.)</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p><m>r</m> is the annual interest rate in decimal form</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p><m>n</m> is the number of deposits (and compounding periods) per year</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p><m>t</m> is the number of years</p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </assemblage>
    <example xml:id="ex-future-value-annuity-1">
      <statement>
      <p>
        A traditional individual retirement account (IRA)<idx>individual retirement account (IRA)</idx> is a special type of retirement account in which income taxes on the money you invest and its earnings are deferred until you withdraw it. If you deposit $100 each month into an IRA earning 6% APR, how much will you have in the account after 20 years? 
      </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
      
      <p>
        In this example the monthly deposit is <m>d=\$100</m>, and since deposits are monthly (and, following the <xref ref="assemblage-finance-compounding-tip" text="custom">tip</xref> above, so is compounding), <m>n = 12</m>. The annual interest rate is <m>r=0.06</m>, and since we want the amount after 20 years, <m>t=20</m>. The unknown value is <m>A</m>, which is the value we need to compute. Putting these values into the annuity equation we have:
      </p>
      <p>
        <md>
        <mrow> A\amp= \$100\left[\frac{\left(1+\frac{0.06}{12}\right)^{12\cdot 20}-1}{\left(\frac{0.06}{12}\right)}\right]</mrow>
        <mrow> \amp= \$100\left[\frac{(1.005)^{240}-1}{(0.005)}\right]</mrow>
        <mrow> \amp= \$100(462.040895)</mrow>
        <mrow> \amp\approx \$46{,}204.09</mrow>
      </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        For monetary values, we round to the nearest cent. The account will grow to $46,204.09 after 20 years.
      </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <p>
      Depositing $100 a month for 20 years takes a lot of discipline!  Was it worth it? How much interest did this annuity earn over 20 years?
    </p>
    <assemblage>
      <title>Key Idea</title>
      <p>
        Total interest is always the difference between the account balance and the amount deposited (or paid).
      </p>
    </assemblage>
    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If you deposit $100 each month into an IRA earning 6% APR for 20 years, how much will you have earned in interest? What percentage of the balance is interest? Use the result of <xref ref="ex-future-value-annuity-1"/> to find these values.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          To find the <alert>amount of interest earned</alert>, first calculate the total of all the deposits.
        </p>
        <p>
          <md>\$100(20)(12)=\$24{,}000</md>
        </p>
    
        <p>
          The difference between the total amount and the total of the deposits is the interest earned.
        </p>
        <p>
          <md>\$46{,}204.09-\$24{,}000=\$22{,}204.09</md>
        </p>
        <p>
          The total amount of interest earned was $22,204.09.
        </p>
        <p>
          To find the <alert>percentage of the balance that is interest</alert>, divide the interest by the total balance.
        </p>
        <p>
          <md>\frac{\$22{,}204.09}{\$46{,}204.09}\approx 0.4806,</md>
          or 48.1%. After 20 years, 48.1% of the balance is from interest.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <assemblage>
      <title>A Note on Rounding</title>
      <p>
        If you are using the formulas and round during intermediate steps you will probably have some roundoff error. For this reason, enter the whole expression into the calculator. Alternatively, enter the parts of the equation separately but use the calculator's stored values for subsequent computations. 
        Do not write down rounded intermediate steps and then key them back into the calculator. In the examples in this section we show intermediate values to guide you, but use the unrounded value stored in the calculator to find the final answer shown.
      </p>
    </assemblage>
    <p>
      Occasionally the problem is more complicated. For example, consider a scenario where a large initial deposit is made, followed by smaller monthly deposits.
    </p>
    <example>
      <statement>
      <p>
        You want to jumpstart your saving by depositing $1,500 from your tax refund and then depositing $150 every month into an account that earns 5.5% APR compounded monthly. How much will you have in the account after 30 years?
      </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
      <p>
        This problem is solved by applying the compound interest formula to the initial deposit, and the future value of an annuity formula to the sequence of monthly payments, and then adding the results.  That is,
        <md>
          \text{Total} = A_1 + A_2,
        </md>
        where <m>A_1</m> is the future value of the lump sum and <m>A_2</m> the future value of the payments. For both computations <m>r = 0.055</m>, <m>n = 12</m>, and <m>t = 30</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Computing <m>A_1</m> first using <m>P = \$1{,}500</m>:
        <md>
          <mrow> A_1 =\amp P\left(1+\frac{r}{n}\right)^{nt} </mrow>
          <mrow> =\amp \$1{,}500\left(1+\frac{0.055}{12}\right)^{12\cdot 30}</mrow>
          <mrow> =\amp \$7{,}781.081761</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Next, using <m>d = \$150</m> compute <m>A_2</m>:
        <md>
          <mrow> A_2 =\amp d \left[\frac{\left(1+\frac{r}{n}\right)^{nt}-1}{\frac{r}{n}}\right] </mrow>
          <mrow> =\amp \$150 \left[\frac{\left(1+\frac{0.055}{12}\right)^{12\cdot 30}-1}{\frac{0.055}{12}}\right]</mrow>
          <mrow> =\amp \$150(913.611893)</mrow>
          <mrow> =\amp \$137{,}041.783877 </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Adding and rounding to the penny, in 30 years our account will contain 
        <md>
          A_1 + A_2 \approx \$144{,}822.87.
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-sinking-funds">
    <title>Computing Payments and Sinking Funds</title>
    <p>
      If a specific lump sum is needed in the future, the annuity formula can be used to determine the necessary deposit size to accumulate the required amount. When a business regularly deposits money into an account to save for future equipment purchases, this savings fund is called a <term>sinking fund</term>. To solve these problems, the annuity formula is used to solve for <m>d</m>, the deposit amount.
      <idx>sinking fund</idx>
      <idx><h>annuities</h><h>sinking fund</h></idx>
    </p>
  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Danielle needs $5,000 in three years.  How much should she deposit each month in an account that pays 8% compounded monthly to achieve her goal?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        In this problem we know all the needed values to use the annuity formula except <m>d</m>: <m>A=\$5{,}000</m>, <m>r=0.08</m>, <m>n = 12</m>, and <m>t = 3</m>.  Thus,
        <md>
          <mrow> \$5{,}000 =\amp d \left[\frac{\left(1+\frac{0.08}{12}\right)^{12\cdot 3}-1}{\frac{0.08}{12}}\right] </mrow>
          <mrow> \$5{,}000 =\amp d(40.535558) </mrow>
          <mrow> d =\amp\frac{\$5{,}000}{40.535558} </mrow>
          <mrow> \approx\amp \$123.35. </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Danielle needs to deposit $123.35 at the end of each month for 3 years into an account paying 8% compounded monthly to have $5,000 at the end of the 3 years.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>
  <example xml:id="ex-sinking-fund-1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        A business needs $450,000 in five years to upgrade some equipment.  How much should be deposited each quarter in a sinking fund that earns 9% compounded quarterly to have this amount in five years?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        In this problem, we seek a value <m>d</m> such that
        <md>
          \$450{,}000 = d \left[\frac{\left(1+\frac{0.09}{4}\right)^{4\cdot 5}-1}{\frac{0.09}{4}}\right].
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Solving,
        <md>
          <mrow> \$450{,}000 =\amp d \left[\frac{\left(1.0225\right)^{20}-1}{0.0225}\right]</mrow>
          <mrow> \$450{,}000 =\amp d(24.911520)</mrow>
          <mrow> d =\amp \frac{\$450{,}000}{24.911520} </mrow>
          <mrow> \approx\amp \$18{,}063.93.</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        The business needs to deposit $18,063.93 at the end of each quarter for 5 years into a sinking fund earning interest of 9% compounded quarterly in order to have the $450,000 needed for the equipment upgrade at the end of 5 years.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>
  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        How much interest did the business in <xref ref="ex-sinking-fund-1"/> earn on its sinking fund? What percentage of the final account balance is interest?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        To find the amount of interest earned, first calculate the total of all the deposits.
      </p>
      <p>
        <md>\$18{,}063.93(4)(5)=\$361{,}278.60</md>
      </p>
  
      <p>
        The difference between the final balance and the total deposits is the interest earned.
      </p>
      <p>
        <md>\$450{,}000.00-\$361{,}278.60=\$88{,}721.40.</md>
      </p>
      <p>
        The total amount of interest earned by the sinking fund was $88,721.40.
      </p>
      <p>
        To find the percentage of the balance that is interest, we divide the interest by the total balance.
      </p>
      <p>
        <md>\frac{\$88{,}721.40}{\$450{,}000.00}\approx 0.1972,</md>
        or 19.7%. After 5 years, 19.7% of the balance is from interest.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-derivation-future-value-of-annuity">
    <title>Deriving the Future Value of an Annuity Formula (Optional)</title>
    <p>
      If you are interested in where the future value of an annuity formula came from, we explain it here. A savings plan with regular payments can be described recursively. Recall that basic compound interest follows from the relationship for each compounding period:

      <md>A=P\left(1+\frac{r}{n}\right)</md>
    </p>
  
    <p>
      For a savings plan, we need to add a deposit, <m>d</m>, to the account with each compounding period:

      <md>A=P\left(1+\frac{r}{n}\right)+d</md>
    </p>
  
    <p>
      Taking this equation from recursive form to explicit form is a bit trickier than with compound interest. It will be easiest to see by working with an example rather than working in general.
    </p>
    <p>
      Suppose we will deposit $100 each month into an account paying 6% APR. The compounding frequency is not given, but as stated in the <xref ref="assemblage-finance-compounding-tip" text="custom">tip</xref> at the beginning of the section, since deposits are made monthly we assume that the compounding period is monthly too.
    </p>
    <p>
      In this example:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p> <m>r=0.06</m></p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p> <m>n=12</m></p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p> <m>d=\$100</m></p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
 
    <p>
      Using <m>P_{m}</m> to represent the balance after <m>m</m> compounding periods, the recursive equation is
      <md>P_{m}=\left(1+\frac{0.06}{12}\right)P_{m-1}+100=(1.005)P_{m-1}+100.</md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Assuming we start with an empty account, we can begin using this relationship:

      <md>
        <mrow> P_{0} =\amp 0 </mrow>
        <mrow> \amp </mrow>
        <mrow> P_{1} =\amp (1.005)P_{0}+100</mrow>
        <mrow>       =\amp 100 </mrow>
        <mrow> \amp </mrow>
        <mrow> P_{2} =\amp (1.005)P_{1}+100</mrow>
        <mrow>       =\amp (1.005)(100)+100 </mrow>
        <mrow>       =\amp 100(1.005)+100 </mrow>
        <mrow> \amp </mrow>
        <mrow> P_{3} =\amp (1.005)P_{2}+100 </mrow>
        <mrow>       =\amp (1.005)(100(1.005)+100)+100 </mrow>
        <mrow>       =\amp 100(1.005)^{2}+100(1.005)+100 </mrow>
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Continuing this pattern, after <m>m</m> deposits, we'd have saved:
      <md>P_{m}=100(1.005)^{m-1}+100(1.005)^{m-2}+\cdots+100(1.005)+100</md>
    </p>
  
    <p>
      In other words, after <m>m</m> months, the first deposit will have earned compound interest for <m>m-1</m> months. The second deposit will have earned interest for <m>m-2</m> months. The next-to-last deposit will have earned only one month's worth of interest. The most recent deposit will have earned no interest yet.
    </p>
  
    <p>
      This equation leaves a lot to be desired, though<mdash/>it doesn't make calculating the ending balance any easier! To simplify things, multiply both sides of the equation by 1.005:

      <md>1.005P_{m}=1.005[100(1.005)^{m-1}+100(1.005)^{m-2}+\cdots+100(1.005)+100]</md>
    </p>
  
    <p>
      Distributing on the right side of the equation gives

      <md>1.005P_{m}=100(1.005)^{m}+100(1.005)^{m-1}+\cdots+100(1.005)^{2}+100(1.005)</md>
    </p>
    <p>
      Now we'll line this up with like terms from our original equation and subtract:
      <md>1.005P_{m}=100(1.005)^{m}+100(1.005)^{m-1}+\cdots+ 100(1.005).</md>
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>P_{m} = 100(1.005)^{m-1}+\cdots+ 100(1.005)+100</md>
    </p>
  
    <p>
      Almost all the terms cancel on the right side when we subtract, leaving
      <md>1.005P_{m}-P_{m}=100(1.005)^{m}-100</md>
    </p>
    <p>
      Solving for <m>P_{m}</m>:
      <md>
        <mrow> 0.005P_{m} =\amp 100((1.005)^{m}-1) </mrow>
        <mrow> \amp </mrow>
        <mrow> P_{m} =\amp \frac{100(1.005)^{m}-100}{0.005} </mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      Replacing <m>P_{m}</m> with <m>A</m> (the future value), <m>m</m> months with <m>12t</m>, where <m>t</m> is measured in years, gives

      <md>A=\frac{100\left((1.005)^{12t}-1\right)}{0.005}</md>
    </p>
  
    <p>
      Recall <m>0.005</m> was <m>r/n</m> and 100 was the deposit <m>d</m>. The value 12 is <m>n</m>, the number of deposits per year. Generalizing this result gives the formula for the future value of an ordinary annuity in <xref ref="subsec-annuities"/>.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <exercises>
    <!-- Sources: Exercises 1 through 6 and 13 are adapted from Benjamin
    Jackson's MATH 105 lecture slides, clicker questions, lecture notes,
    and warm-up (Objective 3), Walla Walla University, Fall 2026.
    Exercises 7 and 8 are based on his MATH 105 written-homework question
    library (Fall 2026). Exercises 9 through 12 are new.
    -->
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-finance-annuity-mia-noah"><!--  #1 (Obj 3 warm-up: Mia and Noah, new numbers) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          Mia and Noah each save $4,000 over 4 years in accounts paying 8% interest compounded annually. Mia deposits all $4,000 today. Noah deposits $1,000 at the end of each year, so his plan is an ordinary annuity.
          <ol marker="a)">
            <li>
              <p>
                How much does Mia have after 4 years?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Complete the table to find how much Noah has after 4 years.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Both put in $4,000. Why do they end up with different amounts?
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
        <tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
          <row header="yes">
            <cell>Noah's deposit</cell>
            <cell>Years earning interest</cell>
            <cell>Value after 4 years</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>End of year 1</cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>End of year 2</cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>End of year 3</cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>End of year 4</cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Total</cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <ol marker="a)">
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>\$4{,}000(1.08)^4 \approx \$5{,}441.96</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Noah has $4,506.11; see the table.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                All of Mia's money earns interest for the full 4 years. Noah's later deposits sit in the account for less time, and his last deposit earns no interest at all.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
        <tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
          <row header="yes">
            <cell>Noah's deposit</cell>
            <cell>Years earning interest</cell>
            <cell>Value after 4 years</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>End of year 1</cell>
            <cell>3</cell>
            <cell><m>\$1{,}000(1.08)^3 = \$1{,}259.71</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>End of year 2</cell>
            <cell>2</cell>
            <cell><m>\$1{,}000(1.08)^2 = \$1{,}166.40</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>End of year 3</cell>
            <cell>1</cell>
            <cell><m>\$1{,}000(1.08)^1 = \$1{,}080.00</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>End of year 4</cell>
            <cell>0</cell>
            <cell><m>\$1{,}000(1.08)^0 = \$1{,}000.00</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Total</cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>$4,506.11</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #2 (Obj 3 lecture notes: checking the formula on Noah) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the future value of an annuity formula to find the amount in Noah's account in <xref ref="exer-finance-annuity-mia-noah"/>. Does it match your table?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Here <m>d = \$1{,}000</m>, <m>r/n = 0.08</m>, and <m>nt = 4</m>.
          <md>
            A = \$1{,}000\left[\frac{(1.08)^4 - 1}{0.08}\right] = \$1{,}000(4.506112) \approx \$4{,}506.11
          </md>
          Yes, it matches. The formula adds up the same four deposits in one calculation.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #3 (Obj 3 slides and clicker: Georgia, new numbers) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          You deposit $40 each month into an account paying 4.8% compounded monthly. How much do you have after 8 years? How much did you deposit, how much interest did you earn, and what percent of the balance is interest?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Here <m>r/n = 0.048/12 = 0.004</m> and <m>nt = 12(8) = 96</m> deposits.
          <md>
            A = \$40\left[\frac{(1.004)^{96} - 1}{0.004}\right] \approx \$4{,}670.21
          </md>
          You deposited <m>\$40(96) = \$3{,}840</m> and earned <m>\$4{,}670.21 - \$3{,}840 = \$830.21</m> in interest. Since <m>\frac{\$830.21}{\$4{,}670.21} \approx 0.178</m>, about 17.8% of the balance is interest.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #4 (Obj 3 lecture notes: Georgia rounding) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          Georgia deposits $25 each month into an account paying 6% compounded monthly for 10 years. To find her balance, a student first rounds <m>(1.005)^{120}</m> to 1.82, then computes <m>\$25\left[\frac{1.82 - 1}{0.005}\right] = \$4{,}100</m>. Find the correct balance. What went wrong?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <md>
            A = \$25\left[\frac{(1.005)^{120} - 1}{0.005}\right] = \$25\left[\frac{1.819397 - 1}{0.005}\right] \approx \$4{,}096.98
          </md>
          The student rounded in the middle of the calculation. The small error in <m>(1.005)^{120}</m> was then divided by 0.005 (that is, multiplied by 200) and multiplied by 25, so it grew into an error of about $3. Keep the unrounded value in the calculator and round only the final answer.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #5 (Obj 3 slides: Robert's car) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          Robert wants $10,000 for a new car in 5 years. He plans to make quarterly deposits into an account paying 2% compounded quarterly. How much should he deposit each quarter? How much of the $10,000 will be interest?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Here <m>r/n = 0.02/4 = 0.005</m> and <m>nt = 4(5) = 20</m> deposits.
          <md>
            <mrow>\$10{,}000 =\amp d\left[\frac{(1.005)^{20} - 1}{0.005}\right]</mrow>
            <mrow>\$10{,}000 =\amp d(20.979115)</mrow>
            <mrow>d =\amp \frac{\$10{,}000}{20.979115} \approx \$476.66</mrow>
          </md>
          Robert should deposit $476.66 each quarter. His deposits total <m>\$476.66(20) = \$9{,}533.20</m>, so about <m>\$10{,}000 - \$9{,}533.20 = \$466.80</m> is interest.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #6 (Obj 3 slides and lecture notes: retirement at 65) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          You want $1,000,000 in an IRA when you retire at age 65. The account earns 6% compounded annually, and you make one deposit at the end of each year.
          <ol marker="a)">
            <li>
              <p>
                Complete the table.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                What does the table say about when to start saving?
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
        <tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
          <row header="yes">
            <cell>Start at age</cell>
            <cell>Number of deposits</cell>
            <cell>Deposit per year</cell>
            <cell>Total deposited</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>20</cell>
            <cell>45</cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>30</cell>
            <cell>35</cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>40</cell>
            <cell>25</cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <ol marker="a)">
            <li>
              <p>
                For a start at age 20,
                <md>
                  \$1{,}000{,}000 = d\left[\frac{(1.06)^{45} - 1}{0.06}\right] = d(212.743514),
                </md>
                so <m>d \approx \$4{,}700.50</m> and the total deposited is <m>45(\$4{,}700.50) = \$211{,}522.50</m>. The other rows work the same way with 35 and 25 deposits.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Starting at 20, about $211,500 of your own money grows to $1,000,000; nearly 79% of the balance is interest. Waiting until 40 more than doubles the total you must deposit, because each deposit has less time to grow.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
        <tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
          <row header="yes">
            <cell>Start at age</cell>
            <cell>Number of deposits</cell>
            <cell>Deposit per year</cell>
            <cell>Total deposited</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>20</cell>
            <cell>45</cell>
            <cell>$4,700.50</cell>
            <cell>$211,522.50</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>30</cell>
            <cell>35</cell>
            <cell>$8,973.86</cell>
            <cell>$314,085.10</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>40</cell>
            <cell>25</cell>
            <cell>$18,226.72</cell>
            <cell>$455,668.00</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #7 (based on written 03a) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          Sam opens a Roth IRA with $1,000 and, starting the next month, deposits $75 at the end of every month. The account earns 7% compounded monthly. How much will Sam have after 25 years? How much did Sam deposit, and how much is interest?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Here <m>r/n = 0.07/12</m> and <m>nt = 12(25) = 300</m>. The initial $1,000 grows by compound interest, and the monthly deposits form an annuity.
          <md>
            <mrow>A_1 =\amp \$1{,}000\left(1+\frac{0.07}{12}\right)^{300} \approx \$5{,}725.42</mrow>
            <mrow>A_2 =\amp \$75\left[\frac{\left(1+\frac{0.07}{12}\right)^{300} - 1}{\frac{0.07}{12}}\right] \approx \$60{,}755.38</mrow>
          </md>
          Sam will have <m>A_1 + A_2 \approx \$66{,}480.80</m>. The deposits total <m>\$1{,}000 + \$75(300) = \$23{,}500</m>, so the interest is <m>\$66{,}480.80 - \$23{,}500 = \$42{,}980.80</m>, about 64.7% of the balance.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #8 (based on written 03b) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          A rural fire district needs $750,000 to buy a new fire engine in 8 years. It sets up a sinking fund that earns 4% compounded semiannually. How much should the district deposit every six months? How much interest will the fund earn?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Here <m>r/n = 0.04/2 = 0.02</m> and <m>nt = 2(8) = 16</m> deposits.
          <md>
            <mrow>\$750{,}000 =\amp d\left[\frac{(1.02)^{16} - 1}{0.02}\right]</mrow>
            <mrow>\$750{,}000 =\amp d(18.639285)</mrow>
            <mrow>d =\amp \frac{\$750{,}000}{18.639285} \approx \$40{,}237.59</mrow>
          </md>
          The district should deposit $40,237.59 every six months. The deposits total <m>16(\$40{,}237.59) = \$643{,}801.44</m>, so the fund earns about <m>\$750{,}000 - \$643{,}801.44 = \$106{,}198.56</m> in interest.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #9 (new) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          Ana deposits $2,000 at the end of each year for 10 years, then stops and leaves the money in the account for 30 more years. Ben waits 10 years, then deposits $2,000 at the end of each year for 30 years. Both accounts earn 7% compounded annually, and both end at the same time, 40 years from now. Who ends up with more, and how much did each deposit?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          After her 10 deposits, Ana has
          <md>
            \$2{,}000\left[\frac{(1.07)^{10} - 1}{0.07}\right] \approx \$27{,}632.90,
          </md>
          which then grows for 30 years: <m>\$27{,}632.90(1.07)^{30} \approx \$210{,}348.65</m>. Ben has
          <md>
            \$2{,}000\left[\frac{(1.07)^{30} - 1}{0.07}\right] \approx \$188{,}921.57.
          </md>
          Ana ends up with about $21,427 more, even though she deposited only $20,000 and Ben deposited $60,000.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #10 (new) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          You deposit $200 every quarter into an account earning 5% APR. How much will you have after 12 years? How much of that is interest?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The compounding is not stated, so by the <xref ref="assemblage-finance-compounding-tip" text="custom">tip</xref> at the start of this section, assume it is quarterly, like the deposits: <m>r/n = 0.0125</m> and <m>nt = 48</m>.
          <md>
            A = \$200\left[\frac{(1.0125)^{48} - 1}{0.0125}\right] \approx \$13{,}045.68
          </md>
          You deposited <m>\$200(48) = \$9{,}600</m>, so the interest is <m>\$3{,}445.68</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #11 (new) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          You deposit $20 every week into an account paying 5.2% compounded weekly. How much will you have after 3 years? How much of that is interest?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Here <m>r/n = 0.052/52 = 0.001</m> and <m>nt = 52(3) = 156</m>.
          <md>
            A = \$20\left[\frac{(1.001)^{156} - 1}{0.001}\right] \approx \$3{,}374.70
          </md>
          You deposited <m>\$20(156) = \$3{,}120</m>, so the interest is <m>\$254.70</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #12 (new) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          A student wants $6,000 for a study-abroad semester in 3 years. How much should she deposit each month into an account paying 4% compounded monthly? How much of the $6,000 will be interest?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Here <m>r/n = 0.04/12</m> and <m>nt = 36</m>.
          <md>
            <mrow>\$6{,}000 =\amp d\left[\frac{\left(1+\frac{0.04}{12}\right)^{36} - 1}{\frac{0.04}{12}}\right]</mrow>
            <mrow>\$6{,}000 =\amp d(38.181562)</mrow>
            <mrow>d \approx\amp \$157.14</mrow>
          </md>
          She should deposit $157.14 each month. The deposits total <m>36(\$157.14) = \$5{,}657.04</m>, so about $342.96 is interest.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #13 (Obj 3 warm-up discussion question) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          Two friends each deposit $100 a month into accounts earning the same interest rate, and both stop at age 65. One starts at 25 and the other at 35, so the first friend deposits one-third more money. Without computing, explain whether the first friend's balance at 65 will be about one-third larger, or a lot more than that.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          A lot more. The extra ten years of deposits come first, so they have the longest time to compound, and every deposit that follows also grows on top of a larger balance. For example, at 6% compounded monthly the friend who starts at 25 deposits $48,000 and has about $199,149 at 65, while the friend who starts at 35 deposits $36,000 and has about $100,452, so one-third more money in gives nearly twice the balance.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
</section>